  ":question/id": "c4402732-29b4-541b-a6e6-a372fd33bf97",
  ":question/math-academy-id": "q-141084",
  ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
  ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is false. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""
}
